{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791362318,"nonce":"pEiDlGPtY8uBvK63rdAp/6ERbB0=","message":"Implemented one retained MCJIT engine per LLVMJitBackend, following the private-module ownership pattern in Ash.\n\nEach install resolves external bindings before attaching its module, gives functions/globals/aliases/ifuncs unique install names, resolves entry and OSR helper addresses, then removes the module so its IR can drop. Emitted code and data remain owned by the engine for published-pointer lifetime. Mapped declarations are namespaced too: rebinding a host function or shared global cannot redirect old code. Failed unresolved installs never enter the engine.\n\nFive regression tests cover repeated entry/OSR installs, retained private global data, changing host bindings, rebinding shared globals, failure/retry, and compiling new modules while another thread executes old LLVM code. Passed on macOS ARM64 and NUC x86_64. Existing lazy promotion, LLVM OSR/helper/resume, and OSR tier-transfer tests pass on both. Mac MCJIT probes and five backend unit tests pass. LLVM-enabled conformance: all 46 Lua tests (including official and official_complete) and all 50 Python tests pass on NUC; Mac 43 Lua and 50 Python pass. cargo fmt --all -- --check and git diff --check pass.\n\nPerformance method: baseline 57b6a9167470ed4cbc796d4e2ba40282a1a12e7b versus this change; LLVM enabled and verified in both binaries; interpreter-first tiering and thresholds unchanged. NUC shared benchmark lock, CPUs 0-7, alternating independent processes. All 17 Lua answers and 22 supported Python checksums match reference results. pidigits remains unsupported. Times below are whole-process wall-clock medians; RSS is peak MiB. These are before/after measurements, not new PyPy/LuaJIT comparisons.\n\nLua: five runs per arm initially; selected outliers repeated 15 times per arm. The table uses the 15-run result where available.\n\n| Kernel | Wall before ms | Wall after ms | RSS before MiB | RSS after MiB | Runs/arm |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| fib | 36.4 | 36.5 | 86.7 | 86.4 | 5 |\n| binary_trees | 200.9 | 201.9 | 156.2 | 153.5 | 5 |\n| mandelbrot | 82.8 | 83.0 | 105.0 | 105.2 | 5 |\n| method_call | 130.9 | 131.2 | 106.2 | 106.3 | 5 |\n| nbody | 197.4 | 196.2 | 130.9 | 128.5 | 15 |\n| any_field | 23.4 | 23.7 | 77.2 | 77.1 | 15 |\n| branchy | 98.9 | 98.8 | 87.0 | 87.0 | 5 |\n| collatz | 139.8 | 140.2 | 87.8 | 87.9 | 5 |\n| inlined_call | 37.3 | 37.6 | 87.1 | 87.1 | 5 |\n| free_function_call | 38.5 | 38.6 | 87.2 | 87.2 | 5 |\n| lambda_call | 27.6 | 27.2 | 83.4 | 83.8 | 5 |\n| closure_call | 28.4 | 28.3 | 85.1 | 85.2 | 5 |\n| op_overload | 214.4 | 214.0 | 133.1 | 133.0 | 15 |\n| mandelbrot_strip | 27.3 | 26.5 | 80.4 | 80.2 | 5 |\n| records | 122.8 | 124.4 | 193.1 | 193.1 | 5 |\n| pipeline | 379.9 | 377.0 | 319.7 | 316.3 | 15 |\n| tokenize | 195.1 | 190.1 | 158.3 | 158.3 | 15 |\n\nThe initial apparent op_overload (+12%) and pipeline (+2.3%) slowdowns do not reproduce in 15-run samples. tokenize improves 195.1 to 190.1 ms (2.5%); nbody 197.4 to 196.2 ms. No broad Lua wall-time improvement is claimed.\n\nPython all-kernel sweep: three runs per arm, harness iteration multiplier 5 (kernel-specific scaling retained).\n\n| Kernel | Wall before ms | Wall after ms | RSS before MiB | RSS after MiB |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| ai | 435.3 | 434.4 | 161.6 | 153.0 |\n| chaos | 498.5 | 512.6 | 205.7 | 197.3 |\n| crypto_pyaes | 254.5 | 261.0 | 155.1 | 150.4 |\n| deltablue | 244.5 | 243.7 | 178.7 | 172.0 |\n| fannkuch | 252.7 | 246.1 | 106.9 | 106.2 |\n| float | 109.5 | 107.4 | 134.7 | 133.0 |\n| go | 453.5 | 443.6 | 202.5 | 186.7 |\n| hexiom2 | 2550.1 | 2488.5 | 217.3 | 206.6 |\n| json_bench | 2366.7 | 2363.1 | 161.1 | 156.6 |\n| meteor-contest | 538.9 | 542.4 | 213.3 | 200.4 |\n| nbody_modified | 145.7 | 144.7 | 117.6 | 116.2 |\n| nqueens | 4819.7 | 4777.8 | 220.6 | 208.3 |\n| pyflate-fast | 2617.9 | 2562.2 | 247.0 | 196.3 |\n| raytrace-simple | 1121.4 | 1114.8 | 236.4 | 184.6 |\n| richards | 133.1 | 133.3 | 111.5 | 112.2 |\n| scimark_fft | 497.5 | 496.0 | 194.9 | 192.9 |\n| scimark_lu | 631.3 | 644.2 | 187.8 | 177.5 |\n| scimark_montecarlo | 246.9 | 243.8 | 128.9 | 128.4 |\n| scimark_sor | 8068.6 | 7986.1 | 175.8 | 167.5 |\n| scimark_sparsematmult | 346.5 | 346.0 | 149.6 | 145.1 |\n| spectral-norm | 231.8 | 231.6 | 163.0 | 161.0 |\n| telco | 292.1 | 294.1 | 180.8 | 177.1 |\n\nLarger Python confirmation uses harness iteration multiplier 50. Three runs per arm except richards (5) and crypto_pyaes (25).\n\n| Kernel | Wall before ms | Wall after ms | RSS before MiB | RSS after MiB | Runs/arm |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| fannkuch | 1600.5 | 1604.2 | 107.7 | 106.4 | 3 |\n| crypto_pyaes | 631.4 | 596.8 | 200.7 | 186.2 | 25 |\n| nbody_modified | 338.0 | 335.2 | 123.8 | 122.6 | 3 |\n| richards | 188.0 | 187.6 | 124.4 | 120.2 | 5 |\n| scimark_sparsematmult | 576.7 | 563.8 | 149.6 | 145.1 | 3 |\n\nCrypto has substantial run-to-run variation: earlier 3-run and 5-run batches reversed direction. The final 25-run median is 631.4 to 596.8 ms; means 632.4 to 606.7 ms. Treat it as a variable gain, not a universal 10% improvement. The repeat richards wall median is effectively unchanged.\n\nThe clearest NUC benefit is peak memory: pyflate 247.0 to 196.3 MiB (20.5% lower), raytrace 236.4 to 184.6 MiB (21.9% lower), go 202.5 to 186.7 MiB. Separate Mac peak-RSS-only confirmation (three alternating processes, LLVM on): tokenize 144.55 to 127.16 MiB, nbody 102.36 to 93.12 MiB, pipeline 306.89 to 301.12 MiB. No Mac wall-time claim.\n\nArtifacts on NUC and copied locally: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-engine-lua.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-engine-python-all.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-engine-python-standard.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-engine-repeat-lua.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-engine-repeat-python-standard.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-engine-crypto-python-standard.json. JSON records binary hashes, run counts, individual results and checksums. Conformance log: NUC /tmp/zyntax-nuc-engine-conformance.log. Mac memory artifact: /tmp/zyntax-engine-mac-memory.json.\n\nCompilation still serializes through the existing backend/context mutex. Each promotion still optimizes and emits a fresh private module; batching and continuation prioritization in issue 2708410 remain separate work. Emitted code/data intentionally stay live for published pointers; this change removes repeated engines and retained IR, not all lifetime growth.","files":null}]}